TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    tb277 host NIC eth3   10.38.22.33/27   MAC 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
        |                                   ^
        |  (1) 2000-frame bursts            |  (2) sFlow v5 datagrams (udp/6343)
        |      tools/vsend.py               |      from 10.38.22.34 to 10.38.22.33:6343
        |      each burst has its own --tag |      tcpdump + tools/sflowwire.py
        v                                   |
    port1.0.10   ifIndex 5010   (access port, vlan 10)   <-- THE SAMPLED PORT
  +-------------------------------------------------------------+
  |  swi_d   AT-x960-56YSQ                            <== DUT    |
  |  interface vlan10  10.38.22.34/27                            |
  |  sflow agent ip 10.38.22.34                                  |
  |  sflow collector id 1 ip 10.38.22.33   (port 6343, default)  |
  |  port1.0.10: sflow sampling-rate 50, sflow polling-interval 20|
  |  the other 76 ports: rate 0, polling 0  (the per-port control)|
  +-------------------------------------------------------------+

  One host NIC does both jobs.  eth3 sends the sampled traffic INTO port1.0.10, and eth3 is
  also the sFlow collector that receives the datagrams back OUT of port1.0.10.

  DUT       : swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) console /dev/u2 @115200, S/N A2WQS700B
  host      : tb277 eth3, MAC 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e, 10.38.22.33/27 -> swi_d port1.0.10
  collector : tb277 eth3 at 10.38.22.33, UDP port 6343
  agent     : swi_d vlan10 at 10.38.22.34, configured explicitly and not inferred
  sampled   : port1.0.10, ifIndex 5010 (measured from ifDescr.5010, not assumed).  It was
              never shut and never flapped, because flapping it would break the collector path.
  unused    : swi_b (u3) was not used.  tb277 eth4 <-> port1.0.48 was not used.  No PDU action.
  traffic   : tools/vsend.py, bursts of 2000 x 86-byte IPv4/UDP frames.  Each burst carries
              its own ASCII `--tag` marker in the payload, so a sample can be traced back to
              the burst it came from.
  tools     : tools/sflowwire.py (the sFlow v5 decoder), tools/vsend.py (the bursts),
              tools/qmark.py (read-only CLI enumeration), tools/rc2cfg.py, tools/rcdiff.py.
              All ran on tb277 through tools/tb.

  About the decoder.  `sflowtool` is not installed on tb277, and `tools/` had no sFlow
  decoder, so one was written for this case: `tools/sflowwire.py`.  It reads a `tcpdump -w`
  pcap in pure Python.  Per datagram it prints the agent address, sub-agent id, sequence
  number, agent uptime and sample count.  Per flow sample it prints the sampling rate, the
  sample pool, drops, the input and output ifIndex, and the sampled frame's own header.  Per
  counters sample it prints the ifIndex and the generic interface counters.  `--find TEXT`
  counts the sampled headers whose bytes contain an ASCII marker.
  It was self-tested offline before it was trusted.  A synthetic sFlow v5 datagram was built
  by hand: one flow sample holding a raw header of a known 86-byte frame, plus one counters
  sample.  The decoder returned the agent, sub-agent, sequence, uptime, `sampling_rate=1-in-50`,
  `input=5010`, and the frame's MAC addresses, IP addresses and ports exactly as built.  Only
  then was it pointed at the DUT.

