  Two small notes, neither a finding.  One flow sample carries `input=0`, which means no
  ingress interface: it is a frame the switch itself originated.  The counters sample carries
  a second record of format 2, the ethernet counters, which `sflowwire.py` does not decode.
  Its generic format-1 record is decoded in full.
  => STEP 4 PASS: 2000 frames at a configured 1-in-50 produced exactly 40 flow samples.  Every
     sample reports the configured rate.  38 of 40 decode to the exact frame sent.  The
     sample_pool advances by exactly the rate.  Counters samples arrive at the configured
     interval, and only for the one port that has one.  That is the per-port scope, measured.

STEP 5 -- designed: change ONLY the rate; the yield must change with it        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: set `sflow sampling-rate 500` on port1.0.10.  Change nothing else.  Send the
  identical 2000-frame burst.

awplus# show sflow interface | include port1.0.10
    port1.0.10  500  128  20

    SUMMARY 7 sFlow v5 datagram(s): 3 flow sample(s), 4 counters sample(s);
            agent(s) 10.38.22.34; seq 28..34
    all 3 flow samples report sampling_rate=1-in-500

  40 samples became 3 for the same 2000 frames, when the rate went from 1-in-50 to 1-in-500.
  2000/500 = 4 expected and 3 observed.  That is what "mean sampling rate N" means: the
  sampler takes 1 in N on average.  It is not an exact counter.  Counters samples continued at
  the unchanged 20 s interval.
  => STEP 5 PASS: the configured rate controls the real sample yield, not just the number the
     datagram prints.  A tenfold rate change gave a tenfold drop in samples, and every sample
     reported the new rate.

