  DEFECT 1 of 2 -- sFlow kept sampling and sending after `no sflow enable`.

    SUMMARY 11 sFlow v5 datagram(s): 85 flow sample(s), 0 counters sample(s);
            agent(s) 10.38.22.34; seq 36..46

  Eleven sFlow datagrams carrying 85 flow samples reached the collector after the operator
  disabled sFlow, and after the CLI confirmed it was disabled.  The detail, all from the decode.

  - The samples are of traffic that arrived AFTER the disable.  `sflowwire.py --find`:

        --- s9off.pcap vs CK44309OFF2 ---
        SUMMARY 11 sFlow v5 datagram(s): 85 flow sample(s), 0 counters sample(s); agent(s) 10.38.22.34; seq 36..46; 78 sampled header(s) contain 'CK44309OFF2', 7 do not
        --- s9off.pcap vs CK44309R500 ---
        SUMMARY 11 sFlow v5 datagram(s): 85 flow sample(s), 0 counters sample(s); agent(s) 10.38.22.34; seq 36..46; 0 sampled header(s) contain 'CK44309R500', 85 do not
        --- s9off.pcap vs CK44309ON ---
        SUMMARY 11 sFlow v5 datagram(s): 85 flow sample(s), 0 counters sample(s); agent(s) 10.38.22.34; seq 36..46; 0 sampled header(s) contain 'CK44309ON', 85 do not

    `CK44309OFF2` is the payload marker of the burst that was sent 25 seconds AFTER the
    disable.  78 of the 85 sampled headers carry it.  0 carry the markers of the two earlier
    bursts.  So these are not old samples held in a buffer.  The sampler sampled new frames.
  - They are 85 distinct sampling events, not one buffer re-sent.  The flow sample sequence
    numbers run 45..129, consecutive, continuing the agent's own counter from the enabled legs.
    The start of the first datagram, verbatim:

        #1   11:02:13.277 10.38.22.34:58995 -> 10.38.22.33:6343  sFlow v5  agent=10.38.22.34 sub_agent=0 seq=36 uptime=315542000ms samples=8
            flow sample seq=45 source=0:5010 sampling_rate=1-in-500 sample_pool=4050 drops=0 input=5010 output=0
              1 flow record(s)
              raw-header: protocol=ethernet-iso88023 frame_length=128 stripped=4 header_length=124
                dst=33:33:00:01:00:02 src=00:0c:25:2a:a5:53  ethertype=0x86dd
            flow sample seq=46 source=0:5010 sampling_rate=1-in-500 sample_pool=4550 drops=0 input=5010 output=0
              1 flow record(s)
              raw-header: protocol=ethernet-iso88023 frame_length=86 stripped=4 header_length=82
                dst=02:00:00:00:44:09 src=00:00:5e:00:53:01  ethertype=0x0800  10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.40 proto=UDP  sport=4000 dport=9   <- contains 'CK44309OFF2'

  - The agent's own accounting is wrong too.  `sample_pool` steps by exactly 500 per sample --
    4050, 4550, 5050 ... 46050.  So the agent believes 42,500 packets passed the sampler.  At
    the measured burst rate of 2000 frames in 14.1 s, about 110 frames had entered port1.0.10
    by the time the last of these datagrams was sent.
  - All 11 arrived in one 0.5-second burst, from 11:02:13.277 to 11:02:13.775.  Every one
    carries the same agent uptime, 315542000 ms.  That value independently confirms the capture
    clock: 10:57:50 plus 263 s is 11:02:13.  After that the capture stayed empty for its
    remaining 55 seconds, although the frame burst ran on until 11:02:27.
  - The counters half DID stop: 0 counters samples in 75 s, against one every 20 s while
    enabled.  So the disable took effect on polling and not on sampling.

  Why this matters more than a specification detail: sFlow samples carry the headers of real
  user traffic.  A datagram sent after an operator has switched monitoring off delivers packet
  headers to a collector that the operator believes is no longer receiving them.
  => STEP 6 PASS: the disable worked on the counters half.  On the sampling half it did not,
     on this one execution, and the evidence above identifies the traffic, the sequence
     numbers and the timing.  STEP 7 tested whether it repeats.

